110:## 2.3. Image processing
112:The longitudinal automatic image processing pipeline, developed for NIHPD analysis (Aubert-Broche et al., 2013), was adapted to the scans from the 16p11.2 dataset as described below. The preprocessing steps applied to the native T1w images were (1) denoising, (2) intensity inhomogeneity correction using the N3 algorithm (Sled et al., 1998), and (3) intensity normalization by histogram matching to the ICBM152 template (Fonov et al., 2011). A hierarchical 9-parameter linear registration based on an intensity cross-correlation similarity measure was performed between the T1w images and the ICBM152 template to align the images with the stereotaxic population template (Collins et al., 1994). Brain extraction was achieved using the Brain Extraction based on nonlocal Segmentation Technique (BEaST) (Eskildsen et al., 2012), a multi-resolution, nonlocal patch-based segmentation technique. Subsequently, images were non-linearly registered using the Automated Nonlinear Image Matching and Anatomical Labelling (ANIMAL) algorithm (Collins et al., 1995), a hierarchical, multi-scale registration algorithm. Whole-brain, individual lobes, thalamus, putamen, caudate, globus pallidus and ventricular volumes were calculated, with the right and left volumes combined for analysis.
